{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-345095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=-2{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-2{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=2{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{10}{13})}^{2}+{(\\frac{24}{13})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{676}{169}}=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-345095" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345095" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=4{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{5}{11}.\n$$\n\nFind, in terms of $t$ only, an expression for the **current** $I$ in this circuit, in amperes.\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{5}{11}) \\\\  & =-12{e}^{-3t}+12{e}^{-6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-345120" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345120" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-12e^{-3t}+12e^{-6t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=-2{e}^{-4t}+5{e}^{-t}+\\frac{9}{17}.\n$$\n\nFind, in terms of $t$ only, an expression for the **current** $I$ in this circuit, in amperes.\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-2{e}^{-4t}+5{e}^{-t}+\\frac{9}{17}) \\\\  & =-5{e}^{-t}+8{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-345088" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345088" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8e^{-4t}-5e^{-t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=4{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =-8{e}^{-2t}+6{e}^{-6t}-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-8{e}^{-2t}+6{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{60}{17})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{25}{17})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{4225}{289}}=\\frac{65}{17}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-345096" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345096" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{65}{17}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)={e}^{-2t}-3{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}({e}^{-2t}-3{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-2t}+15{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}+15{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{8}{5})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{36+64}{25}}=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-345094" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345094" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/8cc2afe50a1f1e3ed0a173cdba6f5a5f59145b0afa6038bee7c9a1fe720238a6.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $20\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.125\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.125}=8q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-345115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=4"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+3q=3"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $15\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.1\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/a051a6380df51ba3c1141bc69952ba442dc9c816df30c5640846894fe1ba4c31.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=15\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=15I=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.1}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-345105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{2}q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/a73d5f0c60062001e947301760fe1cdae7e0e17c7fcc9870baf9d0262070b238.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $20\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.025\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.025}=40q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+40q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-345112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+0.08q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+0.08q=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $24\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.05\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/6494f1905ab90cb3d5655aaa824726255dc7332841ec656dc88c2180b0f93a1c.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.05}=20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20q+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-345103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{5}q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-343714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $40\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.005\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-343714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/169844e6d45393119ac1579cc89e34850401810bfcc1c7537d5b72851a0b2b02.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=40\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=40I=40\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=20\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=40\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q+20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-343714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-343714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-343714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=12"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{12}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{10}q=\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{3}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-343714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{3}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/50bb98a563a0ee061a0faa4d4390aaf459a2fdfccb4721d5d7e6ddd3102c98b1.png)\n\nFind the steady-state charge for the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I=12\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+12\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=6.\n$$\n\nThe **steady-state charge** is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response (the part of the solution that depends on the initial conditions) eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n4(0)+12(0)+4q=6,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n4q=6\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{6}{4}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, the inductor acts as a short circuit, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{2}$ coulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-345130" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345130" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $15\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $30\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=6\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/d7145f4b394e7675b87ed851e344756ecb8441ce737f8664fbfd4b0e426ef36f.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/10)}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=6\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=6\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=6\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n6\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=15.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n6(0)+30(0)+10q=15,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n10q=15\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{15}{10}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{2}$ coulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-345127" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345127" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=24.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0,\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the steady-state charge, we substitute these values into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=24\n$$\n\nSubstituting the steady-state conditions gives\n\n$$\n0+4(0)+8q=24,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n8q=24\\,⟹\\,q=3.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current $I=\\frac{dq}{dt}$ has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $3$ coulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-345122" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345122" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345015" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/b885a0dc4bb868284ec96bcbfc964c767fc159aa0728044d722f0f6b11cbfb1f.png)\n\nFind the steady-state charge for the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345015" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $14\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.1\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=14\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=14I=14\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.1}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+14\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=8.\n$$\n\nThe **steady-state charge** is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response (the part of the solution that depends on the initial conditions) eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n4(0)+14(0)+10q=8,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n10q=8\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{8}{10}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, the inductor acts as a short circuit, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{4}{5}$ coulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345015" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-345015" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345015" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $34\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=12\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/d8303b679d4e00bac0f59f51941a2842473e050033f87f56f099b853431ac5aa.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=34\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=34I=34\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/24)}=24q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=12\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=12\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=12\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=12\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+34\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+24q=9.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n12(0)+34(0)+24q=9,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n24q=9\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{9}{24}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{8}$ coulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-345014" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345014" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/1146917884634beea990c281a591257898887a11029f0116e10f8bfd38169282.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $15\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.1\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=15\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=15I=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.1}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+2{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+3(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\, &  \\\\ +2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ \\alpha +3\\beta =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{7}{10},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{7}{10})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}}≈0.71,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-345135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.32"] ["math" "0.50"] ["math" "0.71"] ["math" "0.86"] ["math" "1.00"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.71"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345144" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/42ab81638355355340008eaa54657a0144612ec9d04e44266350cdb8012051de.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345144" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-41\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $30\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.05}=20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-41\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+6(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\, &  \\\\ +4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +18\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-18\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +18\\beta =\\frac{8}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -18\\alpha -5\\beta =-\\frac{41}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5},$ $\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}}≈0.45,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345144" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-345144" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345144" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.22"] ["math" "0.40"] ["math" "0.45"] ["math" "0.58"] ["math" "0.64"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345144" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.45"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-343948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/00602c631bee877e48211dd94a6ea6e1bbb26626f028212c1388ed547f2452f8.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-343948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $105\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{180}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $15\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=105\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=105I=105\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/180)}=180q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=15\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=15\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=15\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=15\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=105\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+180q+15\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+12q=\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+12{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+7(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-21\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(3\\alpha +21\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-21\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 3\\alpha +21\\beta =\\frac{10}{3}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{45},$ $\\beta =\\frac{7}{45}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{45}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{45}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{45}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{45}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{45}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{45}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-3B{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-3B{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{15})}^{2}+{(\\frac{7}{15})}^{2}}≈0.47,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-343948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-343948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-343948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.16"] ["math" "0.23"] ["math" "0.35"] ["math" "0.47"] ["math" "0.53"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-343948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.47"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/7cbcdf7d43a99b9558b1d085d2306c22e1d27134a5b0ea428721ce7bd074b725.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{P}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{Q}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $5\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=5\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=5I=5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q+\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+5(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\ +4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (3\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(-5\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ -5\\alpha +3\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{5}{17},$ $\\beta =\\frac{3}{17}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-4t}-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-5,$ $Q=3,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-5+3=-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-345132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-345132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-343807" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/c8600d487acea6755d032a17a04c1eb584bc1a84888af5ac152001a4a03aa8b1.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-343807" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-65\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $70\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.01\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=70\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=70I=70\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=10\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=10\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-65\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=70\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q+10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{39}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+10{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+B{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+7(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\, &  \\\\ +10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{39}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-21\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(\\alpha +21\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{39}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-21\\alpha +\\beta =\\frac{39}{10}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ \\alpha +21\\beta =-\\frac{13}{2}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{5},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{3}{10}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+B{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+B{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{10})}^{2}}≈0.36,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-343807" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e48a3e4b94e5e58fcc5dd2ac309f33a13cbe5f6a21b952c178f062baf8f45995"} {"question" "q-343807" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-343807" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.13"] ["math" "0.26"] ["math" "0.36"] ["math" "0.54"] ["math" "0.64"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "q-343807" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.36"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b398027133f561052097e1e2cc6f8f3505b8193744147990eb1b0d265b9d808"} {"question" "e-21893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t) = 4e^{-3t} - 2e^{-t} + \\dfrac5{17}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac3{17}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/example-21893.json" "file_sha256" "f69f9dfe4187a7b0d3a1eb014c0dc2d194db4f967cd700a6304d200984666020"} {"question" "e-21893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-t}+\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-12{e}^{-3t}+2{e}^{-t}-\\frac{10}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{6}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = -12e^{-3t} + 2e^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $I_p(t) = -\\dfrac{10}{17}\\sin(2t) + \\dfrac{6}{17}\\cos(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) = -\\dfrac{10}{17}\\sin(2t) + \\dfrac{6}{17}\\cos(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{10}{17}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{6}{17}\\right)^2}\n= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{136}{289}}\n= \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{34}}{17}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/example-21893.json" "file_sha256" "f69f9dfe4187a7b0d3a1eb014c0dc2d194db4f967cd700a6304d200984666020"} {"question" "e-21890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/649a710caaad131ccf79589e91d6059d8be55f2eb86230be6d3b44313a5c733d.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit shown in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The capacitor charge and voltage satisfy $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance (in Farads).*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/example-21890.json" "file_sha256" "bfa48b617934b9306b55fe0281d6d99e2635da07947f914f0225cbb1210d63ca"} {"question" "e-21890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\cos(3t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $18\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\dfrac1{48}\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $6\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 18I = 18\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{(1/48)} = 48q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 6\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n9\\cos(3t) = 18\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 48q + 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $6,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 8q = \\dfrac32\\cos(3t).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/example-21890.json" "file_sha256" "bfa48b617934b9306b55fe0281d6d99e2635da07947f914f0225cbb1210d63ca"} {"question" "e-21891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistance of $30\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitance of $\\dfrac{1}{20}\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductance of $L=10\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The capacitor charge and voltage satisfy $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/example-21891.json" "file_sha256" "421df320c6067d64f4d26977add939f0b5f30ccced55011fedba4d30eb69e2ba"} {"question" "e-21891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram for our circuit is given below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/2f7e02ba534c41bec3783c56a5ce1b94422757f4398af0098db5ba9a0d6ec49e.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{(1/20)} = 20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 10\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10 = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 20q + 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2q = 1.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t \\to \\infty.$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n(0)+ 3(0) + 2q = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n2q = 1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\dfrac12$ coulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/example-21891.json" "file_sha256" "421df320c6067d64f4d26977add939f0b5f30ccced55011fedba4d30eb69e2ba"} {"question" "e-21892" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/94d6100570bdcf98de739edfa18e68e5ba6889ce0ba7cd573cdb07c2cad68b33.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/example-21892.json" "file_sha256" "fda2bb02423f125f2c501682789cc59accb70069623337ab4cdfe413a732cb6f"} {"question" "e-21892" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\sin(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $30\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{0.05} = 20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 10\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\sin(4t) = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 20q + 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2q = 2\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-16\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-16\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))+3(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) &  \\\\ \\,+2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-14\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+(-12\\alpha -14\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-14\\alpha +12\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\ -12\\alpha -14\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{6}{85}$ and $\\beta = -\\dfrac{7}{85}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t} - \\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $q_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)\\approx q_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{6}{85}\\right)^2 + \\left(-\\dfrac{7}{85}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.11,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/example-21892.json" "file_sha256" "fda2bb02423f125f2c501682789cc59accb70069623337ab4cdfe413a732cb6f"}] "usage" {"q-345014" [] "e-21890" [] "e-21893" [] "q-343807" [] "q-345015" [] "e-21892" [] "q-343948" [] "e-21891" [] "q-343714" []} "basis" 2760 "retractions" [[17592186120260 :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12V$, a resistance of $40Ω$, a capacitance of $0.005F$, and an inductance of $L = 20H$. Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*"] [17592186120275 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1845/Source/Images/q-345015.png)\n\nFind the steady-state charge for the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"solving-an-equation-governing-the-charge-in-an-rcl-circuit-sinusoidal-emf\"></a>"] [17592186120274 :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9V$, a resistance of $34Ω$, a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{24}F$, and an inductance of $L = 12H$. Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs."] [17592186120268 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1845/Source/Images/q-343948.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*"] [17592186120263 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1845/Source/Images/q-343807.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*"] [17592186119472 :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n$q(t) = 4e^{-3t} - 2e^{-t} + \\dfrac5{17}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac3{17}\\sin(2t)$.\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit."] [17592186119472 :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t)$, we obtain the following expression for $I$, in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(4e^{-3t} - 2e^{-t} + \\frac{5}{17}\\cos (2t) + \\frac{3}{17}\\sin (2t)) \\\\\n&=-12e^{-3t} + 2e^{-t} - \\frac{10}{17}\\sin (2t) + \\frac{6}{17}\\cos (2t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = -12e^{-3t} + 2e^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The function $I_p(t) = -\\dfrac{10}{17}\\sin(2t) + \\dfrac{6}{17}\\cos(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) = -\\dfrac{10}{17}\\sin(2t) + \\dfrac{6}{17}\\cos(2t)\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{10}{17}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{6}{17}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{136}{289}} = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{34}}{17}}\n$$"] [17592186119469 :question/problem "Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit shown in the diagram above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1845/Source/Images/0caeb32e2a764272e2f5bf55ed6d3cb3.png)\n\n*Hint: The capacitor charge and voltage satisfy $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance (in Farads).*"] [17592186119469 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\cos(3t)\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor of resistance $18\\,\\Omega$, a capacitor of capacitance\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1{48}\\,\\textrm{F}\n$$\n\nand an inductor of inductance $6\\,\\textrm{H}$.\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 18I = 18\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{(1/48)} = 48q$.\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 6\\,\\textrm{H}$. Hence,\n$V_L = 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n9\\cos(3t) = 18\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 48q + 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $6$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 8q = \\dfrac32\\cos(3t)\n$$"] [17592186119470 :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistance of $30\\,\\Omega$, a capacitance of $\\dfrac{1}{20}\\,\\textrm{F}$, and an inductance of $L=10\\,\\textrm{H}$. Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The capacitor charge and voltage satisfy $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*"] [17592186119470 :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is given below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1845/Source/Images/4431ec0a1bf6b5a3df4df83b5784986b.png)\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{(1/20)} = 20q$.\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 10\\,\\textrm{H}$. Hence,\n$V_L = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10 = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 20q + 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2q = 1\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t \\to \\infty$. In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} = 0\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n(0)+ 3(0) + 2q = 1\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n2q = 1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\ncoulombs."] [17592186119471 :question/problem "Find, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1845/Source/Images/07e20c56c0ebb5cdabe4f739d573ddc9.png)\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*"] [17592186119471 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\sin(4t)\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor of resistance $30\\,\\Omega$, a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\textrm{F}$, and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\textrm{H}$.\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{0.05} = 20q$.\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 10\\,\\textrm{H}$. Hence,\n$V_L = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\sin(4t) = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 20q + 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2q = 2\\sin(4t)\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2q_c = 0$.\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n$q_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t}$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t$. So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $4t$, i.e.,\n$q_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t)$.To find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute\n$q_{p}|= α\\cos (4t) + β\\sin (4t),; \\frac{dq_{p}}{dt}|=-4α\\sin (4t) + 4β\\cos (4t),; \\frac{d^{2}q_{p}}{dt^{2}}|=-16α\\cos (4t) - 16β\\sin (4t)$,\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n$(-16α\\cos (4t) - 16β\\sin (4t)) + 3(-4α\\sin (4t) + 4β\\cos (4t)); + 2(α\\cos (4t) + β\\sin (4t))|= 2\\sin (4t); (-14α + 12β)\\cos (4t) + (-12α - 14β)\\sin (4t)|= 2\\sin (4t)$.Equating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n${-14α + 12β = 0\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\cos (4t)) \\\\ -12α - 14β = 2\\end{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\sin (4t))$Solving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{6}{85}$ and $\\beta = -\\dfrac{7}{85}$. Therefore, the particular solution is\n$q_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t)$.\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t} - \\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t)\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The function $q_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\nq(t)\\approx q_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t)\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{6}{85}\\right)^2 + \\left(-\\dfrac{7}{85}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.11\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186118061 17592186118062 17592186118063 17592186118064 17592186118065 17592186118079 17592186118080 17592186118081 17592186118082 17592186118083 17592186118099 17592186118100 17592186118101 17592186118102 17592186118103] "immutable_fields" [[17592186118060 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118060 :answer-field/id] [17592186118060 :answer-field/key] [17592186118060 :answer-field/type] [17592186118078 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118078 :answer-field/id] [17592186118078 :answer-field/key] [17592186118078 :answer-field/type] [17592186118098 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118098 :answer-field/id] [17592186118098 :answer-field/key] [17592186118098 :answer-field/type] [17592186118129 :answer-field/id] [17592186118129 :answer-field/key] [17592186118129 :answer-field/type] [17592186118130 :answer-field/id] [17592186118130 :answer-field/key] [17592186118130 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
